  "worked_solution": "Suppose $I\u2286R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]\u2282I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,2,3,\u2026,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]\u2282[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/assets/084c469b225d2a4cc4bc148fa3a42fb1501141d1adcd91d8d37999409a8d313f.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the point $x=2,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}h(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}h(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $g$ only.\"",
